<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Taqali</h1><p class="st-meta">1530 CE – 1969 CE (approx.) · capital Tasi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim kingdom in the Nuba Mountains of Kordofan whose kings, the meks, kept their throne for over four centuries until 1969.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Taqali arose in the hills of the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-mountains">Nuba Mountains</a> in the early 16th century. Its traditions tell of a Muslim holy man from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> who married into a local ruling family, founding a dynasty that drew on both Islamic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-peoples">Nuba</a> roots, with its seat at Tasi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The meks ruled a mixed population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-peoples">Nuba</a> hill farmers and Arab settlers, protected by rugged terrain. Taqali paid tribute at times to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate</a> and fought off raids from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/musabbaat-sultanate">Musabba'at sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a>. It traded slaves, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley and served as a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuba-mountains">Nuba Mountains</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Taqali survived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turco-egyptian-sudan">Turco-Egyptian Sudan</a> as a tributary; Mek Adam Umm Dabbalo supported the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mahdist-state">Mahdist State</a>. Under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/anglo-egyptian-sudan">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a> the meks served as native administrators. After the 1969 coup, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan's</a> new government dismantled the native administration system, ending the kingdom's official role.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1530 CE – 1969 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:90.15%;width:8.74%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1530 CE – 1969 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tasi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adam Umm Dabbalo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Tegali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Taqali, Tegali</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=taqali" aria-label="Taqali on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5478 1563 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taqali lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5883 1851h0M5886 1850h0M5888 1853h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5798 1819h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5798 1819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Sudan. <a href="/atlas?year=1750&amp;focus=taqali">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

